<chapter xml:id="ch-visualization-diagnostics">
  <title>Visualization and Item Diagnostics</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The previous chapters defined the state, trajectory, processing, and
      baseline-feature layers for drag-and-drop data. This chapter uses those
      features to diagnose items. The goal is still descriptive rather than
      confirmatory: make visible which <xref ref="gt-card" text="custom">cards</xref>
      attract errors, which <xref ref="gt-zone" text="custom">zones</xref> are
      overused, how students move through the state space, and which patterns are
      stable enough to warrant deeper analysis.
    </p>

    <p>
      A useful diagnostic display answers a concrete question about an item. A
      placement heatmap asks where students placed each card. A distance curve asks
      how close states or trajectories were to the <xref ref="gt-key" text="custom">key</xref>.
      A transition network asks which state changes were common. An error-profile
      display asks how wrong placements were structured. The sections below treat
      these four display families in that order, then close with item-level summary
      tables that collect them.
    </p>

    <p>
      These displays can reveal patterns, but they do not explain those patterns
      by themselves. Interpretation still depends on the
      <xref ref="gt-item-design" text="custom">item design</xref>, the
      <xref ref="gt-feedback-policy" text="custom">feedback policy</xref>, the
      reconstruction quality, and any later validation evidence. A few displays
      also preview the label-free and grouping-based comparisons developed in
      <xref ref="ch-group-features"/>; those previews are identified
      as such and should be treated as descriptive diagnostics until the later
      comparison features have been defined.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-placement-heatmaps">
    <title>Placement Heatmaps</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        A placement heatmap summarizes where students placed each card at a chosen
        analysis point. It is the most direct cohort-level visualization of the
        <xref ref="gt-membership-matrix" text="custom">membership matrix</xref>
        representation: one row per zone, one column per card, and each entry equal
        to an empirical placement proportion.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-heatmap-definition">
      <title>Definition</title>

      <p>
        Suppose <m>N</m> students produce checked states
        <md>
          X^{(1)},X^{(2)},\dots,X^{(N)}
        </md>
        for the same item at the same analysis point, such as first check or final
        check. The placement heatmap, introduced as
        <xref ref="an-baseline-placement-heatmap" text="custom">a cohort feature</xref>,
        is the average membership matrix
        <md xml:id="an-diagnostic-placement-heatmap">
          \bar{X}
          =
          \frac{1}{N}\sum_{\nu=1}^{N} X^{(\nu)}.
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The entry <m>\bar{X}_{ij}</m> is the fraction of students who placed card
        <m>c_j</m> in zone <m>z_i</m> at the chosen analysis point. Each column sums
        to one because every checked state assigns each card to exactly one zone:
        <md>
          \mathbf{1}^{\top}_{R+1}\bar{X}=\mathbf{1}^{\top}_P.
        </md>
        Thus <m>\bar{X}</m> is column-stochastic like each individual membership
        matrix, but its entries are proportions in <m>[0,1]</m> rather than zeros
        and ones.
      </p>

      <p>
        Common analysis points include:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>first check</term>, using each student's first submitted state;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>final check</term>, using each student's last submitted state;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>first correct check</term>, using the first correct submitted
              state among students who eventually solved the item;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>incorrect checks only</term>, using checked states that were not
              exactly correct;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>time-normalized or move-normalized states</term>, using states
              sampled at a common relative point in the
              <xref ref="gt-trajectory" text="custom">trajectory</xref>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The analysis point is part of the definition and should always be reported.
        A first-check heatmap diagnoses initial response structure. A final-check
        heatmap reflects feedback, revision, persistence, and stopping behavior.
        Pooling all checks gives still another view, weighted toward students who
        checked more often.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-heatmap-interpretation">
      <title>Reading a Placement Heatmap</title>

      <p>
        Each column of the heatmap describes one card. A concentrated column means
        most students placed that card in the same zone. A diffuse column means
        placements were spread across several zones. The home zone for each card
        should be marked visually or listed alongside the display, since dispersion
        alone does not distinguish shared correctness from shared error.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-cohort-heatmap">
        <caption>
          The placement heatmap <m>\bar X</m>: each cell is the fraction of the
          cohort that placed a card in a zone, with key cells outlined. Dark mass
          on an outlined cell indicates frequent correct placement; dark mass away
          from the outline identifies a common wrong placement. Mass in a
          decoy-slot row shows how strongly that empty slot attracted cards.
        </caption>
        <image source="figures/dnd-cohort-heatmap.png" width="70%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Heatmap of zones against cards, shaded by the fraction of the cohort
              placing each card in each zone, with the key cells outlined.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Useful diagnostic questions include:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Which cards are most often placed in their home zones?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Which cards are often left in the tray?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Which wrong zones attract a particular card?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Are decoy cards frequently placed into authored slots?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Are decoy slots frequently used?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        A heatmap is marginal: it shows one card at a time. It does not show which
        cards were grouped together within the same student's response. Grouping
        structure requires the partition views and pairwise comparisons developed
        later in <xref ref="ch-group-features"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-card-entropy-diagnostic">
      <title>Card Placement Entropy</title>

      <p>
        The placement distribution for card <m>c_j</m> is the <m>j</m>-th column of
        <m>\bar{X}</m>. Its dispersion is the card placement entropy
        <m>H_j</m> of
        <xref ref="an-baseline-card-entropy" text="custom">the cohort-feature
        definition</xref>. Low entropy means students mostly agreed about the card's
        placement. High entropy means their placements were spread across several
        zones.
      </p>

      <p>
        Entropy measures dispersion, not correctness. A card can have low entropy
        because nearly everyone placed it correctly, or because nearly everyone made
        the same wrong placement. The heatmap column distinguishes those cases.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-linear-summaries-from-heatmaps">
      <title>Linear Summaries from Heatmaps</title>

      <p>
        Any linear state feature can be averaged from <m>\bar{X}</m>. For example,
        the cohort's mean agreement score is
        <md>
          \frac{1}{N}\sum_{\nu=1}^{N}\sigma\bigl(X^{(\nu)}\bigr)
          = \bigl\langle \bar{X},X^\star\bigr\rangle_F
          = \sum_{j=1}^{P} \bar{X}_{s^\star(j),j}.
        </md>
        The mean key distance is therefore
        <md>
          \bar{d}^\star
          =
          P-\bigl\langle \bar{X},X^\star\bigr\rangle_F.
        </md>
        When <m>d^\star(X_0)\neq 0</m>, the corresponding mean baseline-corrected
        progress is <m>1-\bar{d}^\star/d^\star(X_0)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Per card, the fraction correct for card <m>c_j</m> is the heatmap entry
        <m>\bar{X}_{s^\star(j),j}</m>. The quantity
        <md>
          1-\bar{X}_{s^\star(j),j}
        </md>
        is the card's empirical difficulty at the chosen analysis point. For a
        <xref ref="gt-decoy-card" text="custom">decoy card</xref>, the home zone is
        the tray, so this difficulty is the fraction of students who placed the
        decoy card into some authored slot.
      </p>

      <p>
        <xref ref="tab-cheat-tallies"/> makes these summaries concrete. It gives
        illustrative placement counts for the four-shape question over <m>N=30</m>
        final checks. Dividing each entry by <m>30</m> gives <m>\bar{X}</m>.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-cheat-tallies">
        <title>
          Illustrative placement tallies for the running example, counted over
          <m>N=30</m> final checks; columns sum to <m>30</m>.
        </title>
        <tabular halign="center" bottom="minor">
          <col width="30%" halign="right"/>
          <col width="10%"/>
          <col width="10%"/>
          <col width="10%"/>
          <col width="10%"/>
          <col width="10%"/>
          <row bottom="medium">
            <cell bottom="none"></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell><p><m>c_1</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>c_2</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>c_3</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>c_4</m></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell bottom="none"><p>Tray</p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>z_0</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>1</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>2</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>1</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>21</m></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell bottom="none"><p>Triangle</p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>z_1</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>25</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>16</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>3</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>1</m></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell bottom="none"><p>Quadrilateral</p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>z_2</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>4</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>12</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>26</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>1</m></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell bottom="none"><p>Pentagon (decoy)</p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>z_3</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>0</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>0</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>0</m></p></cell>
            <cell><p><m>7</m></p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        The key for the example is <m>s^\star=(1,1,2,0)</m>. From the table:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Mean score:</term>
              <md>
                \bigl\langle\bar{X},X^\star\bigr\rangle_F
                =\sum_{j=1}^{4}\bar{X}_{s^\star(j),j}
                =\frac{25+16+26+21}{30}
                =\frac{88}{30}\approx2.93,
              </md>
              so the mean key distance is
              <m>\bar{d}^\star=4-2.93\approx1.07</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Per-card difficulty</term> <m>(1-\bar{X}_{s^\star(j),j}):</m>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  right triangle <m>c_2^1</m>: <m>14/30\approx0.47</m> 
                  (the hardest card),
                </li>
                <li>circle <m>c_4^0</m>: <m>0.30</m> (decoy card),</li>
                <li>equilateral triangle <m>c_1^1</m>: <m>0.17</m>,</li>
                <li>square <m>c_3^2</m>: <m>0.13</m>.</li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Decoy-slot use:</term> the decoy-slot row is
              <md>
                \bar{X}_{3,\cdot}=(0,0,0,7/30),
              </md>
              so the empty Pentagon slot was used for the circle in about
              <m>23\%</m> of final checks.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Decoy-card leak:</term> the decoy-card column gives
              <md>
                1-\bar{X}_{0,4}=\frac{9}{30}=0.30.
              </md>
              Thus <m>30\%</m> of final checks placed the circle into an authored
              slot.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The <term>mean zone confusion matrix</term> <m>\bar{M}</m>, developed
              in <xref ref="subsec-zone-confusion-displays" text="title"/>, carries the same
              information as first moments: 
              <ul>
                <li>
                  mean omissions 
                  <m>4/30\approx0.13</m>,
                </li>
                <li>
                  mean <xref ref="gt-decoy-card-leak" text="title"/>
                  <m>9/30=0.30</m>,
                </li>
                <li>
                  mean <xref ref="gt-decoy-slot-leak" text="title"/>
                  <m>7/30\approx0.23</m>.
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The same counts also give several mean error features. The mean number of
        omissions is <m>(1+2+1)/30\approx0.13</m>. The mean decoy-card leak is
        <m>9/30=0.30</m>. The mean decoy-slot leak is <m>7/30\approx0.23</m>.
        These are first-moment summaries and can be read from the heatmap.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-cohort-difficulty">
        <caption>
          Per-card difficulty: one minus the fraction of the cohort that places
          each card in its key zone. This is the complement of the outlined cells
          in <xref ref="fig-cohort-heatmap"/>.
        </caption>
        <image source="figures/dnd-cohort-difficulty.png" width="65%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Horizontal bar chart of each card's difficulty, sorted with the
              hardest on top.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Nonlinear summaries cannot, in general, be recovered from <m>\bar{X}</m>
        alone. The exact-correct rate <m>\bar{A}</m> depends on each student's
        whole configuration, not only on marginal card-placement frequencies.
        Quantiles, common-state counts, and grouping summaries likewise require
        the per-student states. As a rule, means of placement counts can be read
        from <m>\bar{X}</m>, while rates of whole-response properties and
        distributions must be computed before aggregation.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-decoy-pull">
      <title>Decoys in the Heatmap</title>

      <p>
        The two structural decoys appear as a distinguished row and a distinguished
        column. A <xref ref="gt-decoy-slot" text="custom">decoy slot</xref> is a slot
        empty in the key; its row <m>\bar{X}_{i,\cdot}</m> gives, card by card,
        the fraction of the cohort that placed a card into that empty slot. The row
        sum <m>\sum_j\bar{X}_{ij}</m> is the mean number of cards placed there.
      </p>

      <p>
        A <xref ref="gt-decoy-card" text="custom">decoy card</xref> is a card whose
        home zone is the tray. Its column shows where students placed it. The
        entries <m>\bar{X}_{i,j}</m> for <m>i\ge 1</m> give the fraction who placed
        the card in each authored slot, and the total
        <m>1-\bar{X}_{0,j}</m> is the decoy-card-leak rate. Thus empty-slot pull is
        read across a row, while decoy-card pull is read down a column.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-heatmap-cautions">
      <title>Diagnostic Cautions</title>

      <p>
        Heatmaps are useful precisely because they compress many responses into one
        display, but that compression has limits:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              small samples can make proportions unstable;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              final-check heatmaps are affected by the feedback policy and by
              students' stopping behavior;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              marginal card distributions do not show within-student grouping;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              visible layout effects may influence placements independently of the
              mathematical content;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              first-moment displays do not retain rates, quantiles, or nonlinear
              grouping metrics.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-distance-and-progress-curves">
    <title>Distance and Progress Curves</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Heatmaps freeze a cohort at one analysis point. Distance and progress
        curves add the temporal axis: they show how close checked states or full
        trajectories are to the key across checks or over an attempt.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-check-distance-distribution">
      <title>Checked-State Distance Distributions</title>

      <p>
        For a checked state <m>X</m>, the baseline key distance is
        <m>d^\star(X)=d_{\mathcal{X}}(X,X^\star)</m>. Across a cohort, the
        distribution of <m>d^\star(X)</m> at a chosen check ordinal gives a compact
        performance summary. Since <m>\sigma(X)=P-d^\star(X)</m>, a histogram of
        scores is the same display with the horizontal axis reversed.
      </p>

      <p>
        Common displays include:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>a histogram of first-check distances;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>a histogram of final-check distances;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>side-by-side distance distributions for first and final checks;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>a table or chart of exact-correct rates by check ordinal.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-cohort-score-hist">
        <caption>
          Distribution of the agreement score <m>\sigma</m>, counted over all
          checks and over final checks only. When final-check mass shifts right,
          later checks contain more correct cards than earlier checks.
        </caption>
        <image source="figures/dnd-cohort-score_hist.png" width="70%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Grouped bar chart of how many checks land at each score value, with
              one series for all checks and one for final checks.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-cohort-cock">
        <caption>
          Correct-on-check-<m>k</m>: the fraction of attempts solved exactly on
          their <m>k</m>-th check. A tall first bar indicates many first-check
          solutions; mass at later checks indicates correction after one or more
          rounds of feedback and revision.
        </caption>
        <image source="figures/dnd-cohort-cock.png" width="55%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Bar chart of the fraction of attempts solved at each successive check
              number.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        First-check distributions are especially useful because they are least
        affected by feedback. Final-check distributions measure eventual success
        under the full item environment.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-trajectory-distance-curves">
      <title>Trajectory Distance Curves</title>

      <p>
        For a reconstructed trajectory with states
        <md>
          X_0,X_1,\dots,X_n,
        </md>
        the <xref ref="an-baseline-distance-curve" text="custom">distance curve</xref>
        is <m>d_k=d^\star(X_k)</m>. A decreasing step indicates movement toward
        the key. A flat step indicates a move that does not change key distance. An
        increasing step indicates movement away from the key. Checks can be marked
        on the curve; resets return the state to the
        <xref ref="gt-initial-state" text="custom">initial state</xref>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-cohort-convergence">
        <caption>
          Distance to the key over successive moves, with one faint line per
          attempt, dots marking checks, and a bold line for the cohort mean. A
          steady descent is a direct solve; a rise is a regression; a jump back to
          the starting distance is a reset.
        </caption>
        <image source="figures/dnd-cohort-convergence.png" width="85%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Overlaid line chart of distance against move index for every attempt,
              colored by whether the attempt was ultimately solved, with a mean
              curve.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Individual distance curves support case analysis. Aggregated curves support
        item diagnosis. Aggregation requires an alignment rule: by move number, by
        check ordinal, by first incorrect check, by first correct check, or by a
        time-normalized scale. The alignment rule should be reported with the
        display.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-cohort-small-multiples">
        <caption>
          The same trajectories shown as small multiples, one panel per attempt, so
          the shape of each path remains legible: direct descents, regressions,
          resets, stalls, and incomplete paths.
        </caption>
        <image source="figures/dnd-cohort-small_multiples.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A grid of small line charts, one per attempt, each showing that
              attempt's distance over its moves.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-progress-curves">
      <title>Progress Curves</title>

      <p>
        When the initial state already has some correct cards, especially because
        decoy cards begin in the tray, raw distance can be harder to compare across
        items. The baseline-corrected progress <m>g(X)</m> of
        <xref ref="an-baseline-corrected-progress" text="custom">the baseline
        feature definition</xref> rescales distance so that the initial state has
        progress <m>0</m> and the key has progress <m>1</m>, provided
        <m>d^\star(X_0)\neq 0</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Progress can be negative if a student moves farther from the key than the
        initial tray state. This can happen when a decoy card begins correctly in
        the tray but is later moved into an authored slot.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-post-check-progress">
      <title>Post-Check Progress</title>

      <p>
        Suppose checks occur at trajectory indices <m>k_1,k_2,\dots,k_q</m>. The
        <xref ref="an-baseline-post-check-gain" text="custom">post-check gain</xref>
        from check <m>i</m> to check <m>i+1</m> is
        <md>
          G_i=d^\star(X_{k_i})-d^\star(X_{k_{i+1}}).
        </md>
        Positive values indicate improvement between checked states. Zero indicates
        no net improvement. Negative values indicate that the later checked state is
        farther from the key.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-cohort-learning-curve">
        <caption>
          Mean agreement score at the <m>k</m>-th check. Unlike
          correct-on-check-<m>k</m>, this curve tracks partial progress, so a cohort
          can show improvement even before many students solve the item exactly.
        </caption>
        <image source="figures/dnd-cohort-learning_curve.png" width="70%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Line chart of average score against check number, with the maximum
              score drawn as a dashed ceiling.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Post-check displays help diagnose whether feedback is followed by productive
        revision. This interpretation depends on the feedback policy: detailed
        feedback and all-or-nothing feedback create different opportunities for
        improvement.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-transition-networks">
    <title>Transition Networks</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Distance curves compress each state to one number. A transition network
        keeps the states themselves, or a specified summary of them, and visualizes
        movement through the configuration graph. Vertices are states or state
        summaries; directed edges count observed transitions.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-full-state-transition-network">
      <title>Full-State Transition Networks</title>

      <p>
        Let <m>\mathcal{X}_{\operatorname{obs}}</m> be the set of membership
        matrices observed in a cohort of reconstructed trajectories. For observed
        states <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m>, the transition count is
        <md xml:id="an-diagnostic-transition-count">
          C_{XY}
          =
          \#\{\text{observed ordinary moves }X\to Y\}.
        </md>
        This is the same count introduced in
        <xref ref="an-baseline-transition-count" text="custom">the cohort-feature
        section</xref>, now viewed as an edge weight.
      </p>

      <p>
        The directed graph with vertex set <m>\mathcal{X}_{\operatorname{obs}}</m>
        and edge weights <m>C_{XY}</m> is the full-state transition network. It
        shows which configurations are common and which moves connect them.
      </p>

      <p>
        For small items, the full-state network may be readable. For larger items,
        the possible state space has size <m>(R+1)^P</m>, and the observed network
        can still be too large or sparse to display directly. Filtered and
        coarsened networks are usually more useful in that setting.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-filtered-transition-networks">
      <title>Filtered Networks</title>

      <p>
        A filtered transition network retains only states or edges satisfying a
        stated criterion. Common filters include:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>states visited by at least a fixed number of students;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>transitions observed at least a fixed number of times;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>states within a fixed distance of the key;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>states occurring before the first check;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>states occurring after an incorrect check.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Filtering improves readability but changes the object being displayed. The
        filter rule and threshold should be reported with the visualization.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-coarsened-transition-networks">
      <title>Coarsened Networks</title>

      <p>
        A coarsened transition network replaces each full state by a state summary.
        Possible summaries include:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>key distance <m>d^\star(X)</m>;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>progress <m>g(X)</m>;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>error profile <m>\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}(X)</m>;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p><xref ref="gt-occupancy" text="custom">occupancy</xref> vector;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>check or feedback status.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Coarsened networks are easier to read and often more stable across cohorts,
        but they discard information. Many distinct configurations can have the
        same distance, occupancy, or error profile. Coarsened displays should
        therefore be used for diagnosis rather than exact reconstruction. Distance
        coarsening also provides the descriptive bridge to the process models in
        <xref ref="subsec-markov" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-transition-network-questions">
      <title>Diagnostic Questions</title>

      <p>
        Transition networks are useful for answering questions such as:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>Which wrong states are common hubs?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Which moves are common immediately before the first check?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Which moves are common immediately after feedback?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Do students tend to move monotonically toward the key or cycle among nearby states?</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Are resets associated with particular regions of the state space?</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-error-profile-displays">
    <title>Error Profile Displays</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Distance displays count how many cards are wrong. Error-profile displays
        show how the wrong cards are wrong: left in the tray, placed in the wrong
        slot, or involved in a decoy error. They visualize the structural error
        features of the baseline chapter at the cohort level.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-error-profile-bars">
      <title>Error Profile Bar Charts</title>

      <p>
        For a checked state <m>X</m>, the baseline error profile
        <m>\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}(X)</m> collects omissions,
        wrong-slot placements, decoy-card leaks, and decoy-slot leaks. For a cohort
        of checked states, the total for displayed component <m>r</m> is
        <md xml:id="an-diagnostic-mean-error-feature">
          E_r
          =
          \sum_{\nu=1}^{N} \varepsilon_r\bigl(X^{(\nu)}\bigr),
        </md>
        where <m>\varepsilon_r</m> denotes that component of the baseline error
        profile. Dividing by <m>N</m> gives the corresponding mean per checked
        state.
      </p>

      <p>
        The four baseline bars should be read with care. Omissions, wrong-slot
        placements, and decoy-card leaks form the additive decomposition of
        <m>d^\star(X)</m>; decoy-slot leaks are an overlapping structural flag, not
        a fourth part of the distance. If the next chapter's grouping features are
        added to the display, their units should be labeled explicitly because pair
        counts and label-free grouping diagnostics are not card-count components of
        the baseline distance.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-cohort-errors">
        <caption>
          Error-profile totals over the checked states in scope. The baseline bars
          separate omissions, wrong-slot placements, decoy-card leaks, and decoy-slot
          leaks. Optional advanced bars can add grouping failures after the
          label-free and partition-based features have been defined.
        </caption>
        <image source="figures/dnd-cohort-errors.png" width="70%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Bar chart of error-type counts summed across checked states.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-zone-confusion-displays">
      <title>Zone Confusion Displays</title>

      <p>
        The zone confusion matrix <m>M(X)=XX^{\star\top}</m> records where cards
        are relative to their home zones. For a cohort of checked states, the mean
        zone confusion matrix is
        <md xml:id="an-diagnostic-mean-zone-confusion">
          \bar{M}
          =
          \frac{1}{N}\sum_{\nu=1}^{N} M\bigl(X^{(\nu)}\bigr).
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The entry <m>\bar{M}_{ih}</m> is the average number of cards currently in
        zone <m>z_i</m> whose home zone is <m>z_h</m>. Diagonal entries represent
        agreement with the key. Off-diagonal entries identify common source-target
        confusions. The row and column margins give the mean current occupancy and
        the key occupancy, as in the single-state confusion matrix.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-cohort-confusion">
        <caption>
          Zone confusion from the key's point of view. Each row corresponds to a key
          group, and each column corresponds to the zone where those cards were
          placed. Diagonal mass is correct placement; off-diagonal mass identifies
          common category confusions; mass in a decoy-slot column is leakage into a
          slot the key leaves empty.
        </caption>
        <image source="figures/dnd-cohort-confusion.png" width="55%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Row-normalized heatmap of key group against the zone where the cohort
              actually placed those cards.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <xref ref="fig-cohort-confusion"/> presents the same information after
        normalizing each key group by its key occupancy. This form is especially
        useful when an item has several categories, because it shows whether errors
        concentrate in particular wrong zones or spread broadly across the response
        space.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-decoy-diagnostic-displays">
      <title>Decoy Diagnostics</title>

      <p>
        Items with decoy cards or decoy slots should include decoy-specific
        diagnostics. The heatmap row-and-column reading of
        <xref ref="subsec-decoy-pull" text="title"/> gives the marginal rates.
        Additional useful displays include:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>the proportion of students who placed each decoy card into any slot;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>the distribution of zones chosen for each decoy card;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>the proportion of students who used each decoy slot;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>the joint frequency of decoy-card leaks and decoy-slot leaks.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        These displays distinguish a decoy that is rarely used from one that
        consistently attracts responses. Consistent attraction may be useful when
        the decoy is intentionally diagnostic, but problematic when it reflects an
        unintended prompt, layout, or wording effect.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-error-profile-by-check">
      <title>Error Profiles Across Checks</title>

      <p>
        Error profiles can be computed separately by check ordinal. For example,
        the mean decoy-card leak at first check can be compared with the mean
        decoy-card leak at final check. A large decrease suggests that feedback and
        revision reduced decoy-card placement. Little or no decrease suggests that
        the error persisted through revision.
      </p>

      <p>
        As with all post-feedback summaries, the feedback policy should be reported.
        Detailed feedback can change error profiles in ways that belong to the item
        environment, not only to students' initial responses.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-mislabel-gap-preview">
      <title>Previewing Label-Free Comparison</title>

      <p>
        One diagnostic question looks past authored labels. The baseline features
        treat a card as correct only in its exact home zone, but a student may group
        the cards correctly while assigning those groups to the wrong slot names.
        The next chapter defines a label-free agreement score and the corresponding
        labeling gap that separate grouping errors from naming errors. A histogram
        of that gap is useful when an item has interchangeable-looking or easily
        swapped category labels.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is only a preview. Until the label-free comparison has been defined,
        the baseline agreement score <m>\sigma(X)</m> and key distance
        <m>d^\star(X)</m> remain the operative correctness measures in this
        chapter.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-item-level-diagnostic-summaries">
    <title>Item-Level Diagnostic Summaries</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The displays above examine one item closely. Item-level summaries collect
        the most important diagnostic features into a compact table. They support
        item comparison, unusual-pattern detection, and selection of items for
        deeper qualitative or statistical study.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-item-diagnostic-table">
      <title>Diagnostic Summary Table</title>

      <p>
        A minimal item-level diagnostic table should combine design covariates,
        outcome summaries, process summaries, error summaries, dispersion summaries,
        and quality-control summaries.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-item-diagnostic-summary-fields">
        <title>Suggested fields for an item-level diagnostic summary.</title>
        <tabular valign="top" bottom="minor">
          <col width="28%" halign="left"/>
          <col width="72%" halign="left"/>
          <row bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Field family</p></cell>
            <cell><p>Examples</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>design</p></cell>
            <cell>
              <p>
                <m>P</m>, <m>R</m>, number of decoy cards, number of decoy slots,
                feedback policy
              </p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>outcome</p></cell>
            <cell>
              <p>
                first-check correct rate, eventual correct rate, mean first-check
                distance, mean final-check distance
              </p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>process</p></cell>
            <cell>
              <p>
                mean number of moves, mean number of checks, reset rate, median
                active time, median time to first check
              </p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>error profile</p></cell>
            <cell>
              <p>
                mean omissions, wrong-slot placements, decoy-card leaks,
                decoy-slot leaks
              </p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>dispersion</p></cell>
            <cell>
              <p>
                mean card entropy, maximum card entropy, number of common wrong states
              </p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>quality control</p></cell>
            <cell>
              <p>
                number of attempts, number passing replay checks, number with parse
                errors, number with long idle gaps
              </p>
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-diagnostic-flags">
      <title>Item Diagnostic Flags</title>

      <p>
        Diagnostic flags identify items that may deserve closer inspection. Examples
        include:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>high first-check distance but high eventual correctness;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>low first-check distance but many checks before success;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>high decoy-card leak rate;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>high decoy-slot usage;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>high reset rate;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>many repeated states or cycles;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>high reconstruction mismatch rate.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        These flags are prompts for review, not automatic conclusions. A high
        decoy-card leak rate may indicate a strong decoy, a confusing prompt, a
        layout effect, or an intended diagnostic challenge. The display identifies
        the pattern; interpretation requires returning to the item.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-cross-course-and-semester-stability">
      <title>Cross-Course and Cross-Semester Stability</title>

      <p>
        When data are available from multiple courses or semesters, the same
        diagnostic summaries can be computed separately by cohort. Stable patterns
        across cohorts are stronger evidence of item-level structure than patterns
        appearing in only one offering.
      </p>

      <p>
        Useful comparisons include:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>first-check correct rate by semester;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>placement heatmaps by course;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>decoy usage rates by semester;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>mean distance curves by cohort;</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>reconstruction quality by data source or platform version.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Cross-cohort comparisons should account for changes in item design,
        feedback policy, source version, course context, and student population.
        Apparent differences across semesters may reflect any of these factors.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-diagnostic-to-research-transition">
      <title>From Diagnostics to Research Questions</title>

      <p>
        Diagnostic displays are useful for exploratory work because they locate
        patterns worth studying. A heatmap may reveal that one card is commonly
        placed in a particular wrong zone. A transition network may show a common
        post-feedback repair path. A cross-semester comparison may show that an
        error pattern is stable across cohorts.
      </p>

      <p>
        Such observations can motivate research questions, but they are not
        research findings by themselves. The next chapter,
        <xref ref="ch-group-features"/>, extends the comparison
        vocabulary previewed here with label-free agreement, partition views, merge
        and split features, and pairwise grouping indices. The statistical models of
        <xref ref="ch-statistical-modeling"/> and the validity arguments of
        <xref ref="ch-validity-interpretation"/> then address whether a diagnostic
        pattern supports a claim about item design, student behavior, feedback use,
        or learning.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
